<Writeup
  title="WisprFree"
  description="A privacy-first voice-dictation tool that runs entirely offline — Whisper transcription cleaned up by a local LLM, with a personal dictionary, per-app tone profiles, and fine-tuning on your own corrections."
  meta="Personal project · In active development"
  tags={['Python', 'Whisper', 'Local LLMs', 'Electron', 'FastAPI']}
  links={links}
>
  <p>
    Every good dictation tool I tried wanted my audio in someone else's cloud. WisprFree is my
    answer: press a hotkey anywhere in the OS, talk, and clean text appears at your cursor —
    with nothing ever leaving the machine. No accounts, no telemetry, and the API daemon binds to
    localhost only.
  </p>

  <h2>Two-stage pipeline</h2>
  <p>
    Raw speech-to-text output is rarely what you meant to write. WisprFree splits the problem in
    two:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Transcribe.</strong> Whisper (via faster-whisper or whisper.cpp) converts audio to
      text, seeded with a personal dictionary so names, jargon, and acronyms come out right the
      first time.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Refine.</strong> A local LLM served by Ollama strips filler words, fixes grammar,
      resolves mid-sentence self-corrections ("send it Tuesday — no, Wednesday"), and adapts tone to
      the app you're dictating into.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The parts I'm proud of</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Personal dictionary</strong> — a SQLite store of your vocabulary that biases both
      stages. When the same correction keeps happening, WisprFree notices and suggests adding the
      term.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Context awareness</strong> — it detects the active application (AppKit on macOS, Win32
      on Windows, X11/Hyprland/Sway on Linux) and switches between casual, formal, and technical
      tone profiles accordingly. Slack-you and email-you are different people.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It learns your voice</strong> — corrections are logged locally and can be used to
      LoRA-fine-tune both stages: the cleanup model from your correction history, and Whisper itself
      from recordings of you reading phonetically balanced sentences, with a held-out set to report
      the actual word-error-rate improvement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>OS-native text injection</strong> — CGEvent on macOS, SendInput on Windows,
      xdotool/wtype on Linux, so it works in any app, not just a browser extension's sandbox.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The system runs as a Python daemon (FastAPI) with an Electron menu-bar app on top for settings,
    dictionary editing, tone profiles, and correction history. Voice activity detection trims
    silence before transcription; dictation audio lives only in memory unless you're intentionally
    recording training data.
  </p>
</Writeup>
